[SPEAKER_01]: You feel fully justified spending your time, you know, studying etymology and stuff, but not for purpose getting the meaning, but for purpose just becoming a scholar.

366
00:27:45,358 --> 00:27:53,561
[SPEAKER_01]: But you feel, again, oh, you know, spending five days, a few weeks, just printing the random CDs because they're all about the dhamma or whatever else.

367
00:27:54,041 --> 00:27:55,682
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you're doing, you know... Publications.

368
00:27:55,802 --> 00:27:56,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Publications.

369
00:27:56,523 --> 00:27:58,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you feel full of joy, because this is good work.

370
00:27:58,504 --> 00:27:59,345
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dhamma work.

371
00:27:59,805 --> 00:28:01,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it for a completely wrong reason.

372
00:28:01,086 --> 00:28:06,871
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it because if you were to be put in the middle of the jungle right here, you would probably freak out.

373
00:28:07,911 --> 00:28:15,457
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that doesn't worry you, the fact that you're not able to exist anywhere in peace, well, that means you're not really practicing correctly.

374
00:28:16,297 --> 00:28:16,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at all.

375
00:28:17,178 --> 00:28:18,079
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how much you learn.

376
00:28:18,099 --> 00:28:19,720
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how many years you spent

377
00:28:21,692 --> 00:28:26,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Dealing and working around and about the dhamma, you haven't got anything from it.

378
00:28:40,025 --> 00:28:45,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what is... Yeah, because you gave this idea of the right efforts.

379
00:28:46,830 --> 00:28:49,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandon and wholesome, cultivate to wholesome, you see.

380
00:28:51,342 --> 00:28:55,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But the idea comes across as, you've got to be doing something.

381
00:28:56,585 --> 00:28:57,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing is... Duty.

382
00:28:57,726 --> 00:29:04,871
[SPEAKER_00]: You go, learn the suttas, you do, you practice... Sīlabhāt, parmasa, holding on to virtue and duty.

383
00:29:06,032 --> 00:29:13,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, not just rites and rituals, but duty, the rightful duty, duty of me as a monk, the duty, holding on to it.

384
00:29:14,858 --> 00:29:15,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing.

385
00:29:15,878 --> 00:29:18,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Holding on to it means doing it for the wrong reasons.

386
00:29:19,422 --> 00:29:21,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, initially, you have to hold on.

387
00:29:21,443 --> 00:29:23,704
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why vinaya or sīla comes first.

388
00:29:24,304 --> 00:29:24,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You take it.

389
00:29:25,225 --> 00:29:25,805
[SPEAKER_01]: You hold it.

390
00:29:26,305 --> 00:29:34,109
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, within that, you're supposed to develop discernments that then makes you let it go, without actually, externally, breaking it, necessarily.

391
00:29:35,170 --> 00:29:41,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Letting go of unwholesome and letting go of wholesome, for an ārahant, doesn't mean he goes and becomes wild or whatever else.

392
00:29:43,472 --> 00:29:59,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that sense of duty, yeah, because that's quite risky, because your whole existence as a monk, or as somebody who practices the Buddha's teaching, that you invested 20 years in practicing dhamma, doing retreats or whatever else, there is that sense of duty that has been built up.

393
00:29:59,742 --> 00:30:10,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And now letting go of that would basically expose your entire existence since you became a monk, your entire, basically, investment, your entire life.

394
00:30:11,153 --> 00:30:12,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why many people never do it.

395
00:30:12,573 --> 00:30:20,956
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's like people who try to cross the shore, but are too afraid, and they stay.

396
00:30:20,996 --> 00:30:26,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they came to the shore, they found what they need to cross, but they're too afraid to actually start crossing it.

397
00:30:27,418 --> 00:30:33,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they just stay in that shore, looking at the other shore they're supposed to go, and they're often happy with that.

398
00:30:34,805 --> 00:30:47,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no matter how much, how many years you spent cultivating and preparing, when you start crossing that river, it's not a guarantee that you will get to the other side.

399
00:30:53,312 --> 00:30:55,453
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is the risk that needs to be taken.

400
00:30:55,693 --> 00:30:56,153
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't

401
00:30:56,962 --> 00:31:00,484
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't then say, no, because if I try, I might fail.

402
00:31:01,084 --> 00:31:06,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I better not try and then just remain concerned about trying, but never do so.

403
00:31:07,287 --> 00:31:14,411
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like people who spend their life studying about the dhamma, the scholarly aspect.

404
00:31:14,511 --> 00:31:19,914
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean all the scholars do that, but many would do it for those reasons.

405
00:31:19,994 --> 00:31:24,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you end up revolving around it, but never really getting through with it.

406
00:31:25,857 --> 00:31:26,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Connecting.

407
00:31:26,730 --> 00:31:27,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

408
00:31:27,430 --> 00:31:30,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Collecting information from this teacher and teacherless.

409
00:31:31,092 --> 00:31:33,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Learning about the other show, second-hand.

410
00:31:41,996 --> 00:31:45,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing right effort.

411
00:31:45,638 --> 00:31:46,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Right effort.

412
00:31:47,058 --> 00:31:47,678
[SPEAKER_00]: How to do that?

413
00:31:47,719 --> 00:31:48,219
[SPEAKER_00]: What is there?

414
00:31:49,719 --> 00:31:54,181
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily about doing things full-on with heavy energy.

415
00:31:54,201 --> 00:31:56,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the right effort will be determined by the right view.

416
00:31:56,899 --> 00:32:03,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the right view always comes first.

417
00:32:03,244 --> 00:32:06,827
[SPEAKER_01]: The right effort would be... Again, it's what I was speaking about.

418
00:32:07,208 --> 00:32:10,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Make sure... Well, right effort, right intention.

419
00:32:10,891 --> 00:32:23,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Right effort would come from right intention, which comes from right view, and right view would be making sure the things you're about to do, things you want to do, things you feel you need to do, are not rooted in the unwholesome.

420
00:32:25,423 --> 00:32:27,164
[SPEAKER_01]: There are duties you just have to do.

421
00:32:28,125 --> 00:32:34,729
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, that doesn't mean you don't... you shouldn't be mindful of them, or you should feel justified in doing them.

422
00:32:35,810 --> 00:32:37,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there are things I need to do.

423
00:32:38,412 --> 00:32:45,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Bottom line is, I always choose to do these things, even if it feels, oh, I need to do this.

424
00:32:45,236 --> 00:32:51,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you are the one who chooses that to be that which you need to do.

425
00:32:51,140 --> 00:32:52,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you make it effortless?

426
00:32:53,450 --> 00:32:58,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a feeling, like it's kind of like you doing it, you kind of... Well, no, see...

427
00:32:58,953 --> 00:33:00,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Intentions to do it themselves.

428
00:33:00,595 --> 00:33:03,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that would be the case for an arahant.

429
00:33:04,777 --> 00:33:08,980
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's the thing, like, for putrījana, that would mean, like,

430
00:33:09,923 --> 00:33:10,743
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not there.

431
00:33:11,143 --> 00:33:12,424
[SPEAKER_01]: These things are just done.

432
00:33:13,284 --> 00:33:15,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And on paper, that can be said to be true.

433
00:33:15,745 --> 00:33:20,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But that can be achieved only through fully doing them, so to speak.

434
00:33:20,646 --> 00:33:29,229
[SPEAKER_01]: And by fully doing them, I mean fully, thoroughly accepting responsibility for every slightest intention you choose to act upon.

435
00:33:29,977 --> 00:33:30,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the other way.

436
00:33:30,818 --> 00:33:35,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by kind of finding a state where you forget about your intentions and then things just happen.

437
00:33:35,882 --> 00:33:37,303
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's completely the opposite.

438
00:33:38,204 --> 00:33:48,253
[SPEAKER_01]: It might kind of revolve around full circle and externally almost look the same, but there is this, on the inner side, there is this whole trajectory of a set that you actually have to go through with.

439
00:33:49,077 --> 00:33:54,000
[SPEAKER_01]: And you go through that kutujana stance by fully accepting responsibility for everything he does.

440
00:33:54,080 --> 00:33:54,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

441
00:33:54,380 --> 00:33:58,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he chooses to do even things that he has no choice about, so to speak.

442
00:33:58,883 --> 00:34:04,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that happen to you, happen to you because you've taken, you've chosen to keep that

443
00:34:06,071 --> 00:34:07,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, other things can affect.

444
00:34:07,792 --> 00:34:11,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, misfortunes, accidents, that are not really your fault.

445
00:34:11,634 --> 00:34:18,898
[SPEAKER_01]: If they affect you, well, that's a new why, because you're holding on to the body that is subject to misfortunes, subject to accidents, and so on.

446
00:34:19,659 --> 00:34:22,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you take full responsibility even for things that you cannot possibly control.

447
00:34:23,061 --> 00:34:25,803
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so hard and heavy, especially in the beginning.

448
00:34:26,583 --> 00:34:33,988
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, by fully accepting that responsibility, you literally go through that responsibility without abandoning it, and then you find a way out, in case of another hunt.

449
00:34:34,973 --> 00:34:44,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby it... while it does remain, it's a pure individual, pūgala, just the five aggregates free from upādāna, and they just rise and fall until they break apart.

450
00:34:45,606 --> 00:34:50,872
[SPEAKER_01]: But that can be done only when you actually go through with the whole thing.

451
00:34:51,954 --> 00:35:00,809
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, unwholesome needs to be avoided, not just, oh, I keep the precepts, but even mentally.

452
00:35:01,209 --> 00:35:05,437
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if it's not really... if it's not really morally wrong,

453
00:35:06,091 --> 00:35:20,599
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that I was about to go to library, because I was becoming a bit anxious in my room, so I needed a distraction, that is... that is like, like, you know, shooting yourself in the head, from the point of view of dharma practice.

454
00:35:21,079 --> 00:35:22,440
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a serious mistake.

455
00:35:23,261 --> 00:35:32,226
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can... you'll be impeccable externally, perfect monk's form, and never break any rules, but you keep doing things like that, you'll never ever progress in any way.

456
00:35:33,169 --> 00:35:39,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're just not really looking at the root, at the core of the problem.

457
00:35:39,171 --> 00:35:45,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you said the other day, you're resisting the possibility of suffering, of pain.

458
00:35:46,233 --> 00:35:52,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when there is no pain, there is a possibility of pain, which hurts you, basically, and you're acting upon that.

459
00:35:53,276 --> 00:35:54,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're acting because you're afraid.

460
00:35:55,356 --> 00:35:56,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're acting out of unwholesome.

461
00:35:58,017 --> 00:36:00,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of going part of

462
00:36:03,600 --> 00:36:07,622
[SPEAKER_00]: And the possibility of pain starts becoming more of a reality.

463
00:36:07,642 --> 00:36:08,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have to deal with it.

464
00:36:09,603 --> 00:36:14,866
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole... That's why that sutta that the Buddha said when he was in the... in that jungle, like...

465
00:36:14,946 --> 00:36:15,886
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a fear and dread.

466
00:36:16,407 --> 00:36:17,707
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was called fear and dread.

467
00:36:17,827 --> 00:36:18,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

468
00:36:18,868 --> 00:36:26,992
