540
00:41:05,421 --> 00:41:09,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't help by having a judgment.

541
00:41:09,304 --> 00:41:10,485
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just a natural thing.

542
00:41:10,865 --> 00:41:13,387
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... thinking is a form of judgment already.

543
00:41:14,268 --> 00:41:21,274
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can help is not develop beliefs on account of your judgment, especially when you know that you don't really know.

544
00:41:22,795 --> 00:41:23,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

545
00:41:23,787 --> 00:41:33,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do have... you can't help it, but you make sure that you find the right judgment, samādhi, the right view, and then you use that as a criteria.

546
00:41:33,716 --> 00:41:35,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, that becomes the criteria.

547
00:41:35,117 --> 00:41:36,398
[SPEAKER_01]: You become that criteria.

548
00:41:53,955 --> 00:41:56,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Back to the room, sitting in your room, bored, bored to death.

549
00:41:57,316 --> 00:41:57,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You're sitting there.

550
00:41:58,836 --> 00:41:59,756
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to practice dhamma.

551
00:42:03,377 --> 00:42:08,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you want to go downstairs to speak to people, to distract, or some various distraction.

552
00:42:08,598 --> 00:42:09,818
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to read a book, a novel.

553
00:42:12,038 --> 00:42:12,859
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't want to.

554
00:42:12,979 --> 00:42:13,659
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do that.

555
00:42:14,419 --> 00:42:15,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're sitting there.

556
00:42:17,099 --> 00:42:17,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain.

557
00:42:18,099 --> 00:42:21,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about all the possibilities, distractions you could have.

558
00:42:24,284 --> 00:42:29,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, all you have is that pain of boredom, that poor, you know, sensuality curve.

559
00:42:31,269 --> 00:42:31,929
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, the best...

560
00:42:32,189 --> 00:42:47,579
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to be... if I've seen right effort, what... to be seen that... seen that that has arisen, that boredom is there, and maybe investigating it.

561
00:42:47,839 --> 00:42:51,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the right effort would be basically not going, not leaving the room, not acting out of fear.

562
00:42:52,502 --> 00:42:53,463
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be the right effort.

563
00:42:55,346 --> 00:43:04,608
[SPEAKER_01]: As to what you do on a more particular level, it would be basically, eh... why... ask yourself why is that whole border not going out?

564
00:43:04,648 --> 00:43:07,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it a... why is it... why is it a problem?

565
00:43:08,109 --> 00:43:09,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it... why is it unwanted?

566
00:43:10,850 --> 00:43:13,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Why does it make you suffer?

567
00:43:13,191 --> 00:43:16,911
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize that it's not about you staying in the room or not staying in the room.

568
00:43:17,051 --> 00:43:18,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about you suffering.

569
00:43:18,972 --> 00:43:19,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to suffer.

570
00:43:19,772 --> 00:43:20,892
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you want to leave the room.

571
00:43:21,693 --> 00:43:22,873
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when I get distracted.

572
00:43:24,074 --> 00:43:26,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is the core of dhamma, suffering and freedom from it.

573
00:43:27,157 --> 00:43:28,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha and freedom from it.

574
00:43:29,919 --> 00:43:33,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do I don't want to be touched by the unpleasant feeling?

575
00:43:33,702 --> 00:43:35,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Why, by default, I resist that?

576
00:43:36,925 --> 00:43:38,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Why, by default, I act upon it?

577
00:43:39,247 --> 00:43:43,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Why I take it for granted that I must run away from this feeling?

578
00:43:44,111 --> 00:43:45,012
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all I have to do.

579
00:43:45,032 --> 00:43:49,415
[SPEAKER_01]: The tiny little thorn needs to be pulled out.

580
00:43:49,856 --> 00:43:52,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a tiny little resistance to pain.

581
00:43:53,985 --> 00:43:56,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You pull that out, you won't be touched by anything.

582
00:43:58,206 --> 00:44:03,987
[SPEAKER_01]: But you certainly won't be pulling anything out if you're running away whenever these things and pressure arises.

583
00:44:04,607 --> 00:44:14,228
[SPEAKER_01]: If you act upon distractions, leave the room, pretend, find dutiful and meaningful sort of things that end up being just distractions or whatever.

584
00:44:14,288 --> 00:44:18,509
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's my duty to spread the dhamma and, you know, do the publication.

585
00:44:18,529 --> 00:44:20,509
[SPEAKER_01]: No, your duty is to become an arahant.

586
00:44:21,730 --> 00:44:23,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, whatever you do, or at least talk about it.

587
00:44:24,124 --> 00:44:29,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, whatever you do will be coming out of the right view, even if externally people don't recognize it as such.

588
00:44:29,628 --> 00:44:30,649
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not your concern.

589
00:44:32,170 --> 00:44:52,665
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a major need for, you know, sitting there, if you're in pain, this fearlessness you need to have, this fearlessness... Basically, the moment, like, the moment you stop fearing the pain, you can get up and leave, if you want to do that, because it won't be rooted in acting out of fear.

590
00:44:53,790 --> 00:44:58,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha wanted to sit down, but he made sure he removed the fear and then he sat down.

591
00:45:01,333 --> 00:45:06,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't possibly not do anything for the rest of your life.

592
00:45:08,096 --> 00:45:12,098
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want to make sure that it's never ever rooted again in unwholesomeness.

593
00:45:13,418 --> 00:45:16,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, there will be quickly remedied.

594
00:45:16,840 --> 00:45:20,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, you might just have to sit in the room and endure it for days.

595
00:45:23,274 --> 00:45:25,595
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, that won't last forever either, so...

596
00:45:27,777 --> 00:45:34,120
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about taking for granted the resistance to unpleasant feeling.

597
00:45:35,081 --> 00:45:36,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That needs to be uprooted.

598
00:45:36,742 --> 00:45:39,444
[SPEAKER_01]: That tiny little thing needs to be pulled out.

599
00:45:40,144 --> 00:45:44,086
[SPEAKER_01]: And then all the other puss and infection on account of it will... it's gone.

600
00:45:44,327 --> 00:45:44,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It will go.

601
00:45:45,087 --> 00:45:45,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually it will.

602
00:45:46,348 --> 00:45:51,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not being, like, you know, infected anymore by that

603
00:45:52,870 --> 00:45:58,574
[SPEAKER_00]: That's sort of the... the craving for the pleasant, yeah?

604
00:45:59,795 --> 00:46:00,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, the same.

605
00:46:00,696 --> 00:46:05,159
[SPEAKER_01]: You crave for pleasure, because basically not having enough of that pleasure is unpleasant.

606
00:46:05,739 --> 00:46:10,803
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your whole life revolves around fear of pain.

607
00:46:12,684 --> 00:46:19,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And even people who are going to subject themselves to pain, to prove themselves that they're not afraid of pain, they need to prove themselves, because underneath that, they're afraid of pain.

608
00:46:20,890 --> 00:46:22,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they try to override it.

609
00:46:30,568 --> 00:46:34,691
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, if you're afraid of pain, then you might start practicing the dhamma.

610
00:46:35,271 --> 00:46:40,115
[SPEAKER_00]: You might, you know, that's my reason for becoming a monk or... That is THE reason.

611
00:46:40,635 --> 00:46:42,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I am... That is the sole reason, yeah.

612
00:46:43,317 --> 00:46:45,079
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subject to suffering.

613
00:46:45,639 --> 00:46:49,982
[SPEAKER_01]: I am... like when a layman reflects before he goes forth in the suttas.

614
00:46:50,843 --> 00:46:52,124
[SPEAKER_01]: I am prey to suffering.

615
00:46:52,304 --> 00:46:52,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Prey.

616
00:46:53,465 --> 00:46:54,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Like I...

617
00:46:54,306 --> 00:46:57,448
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm being hunted, so I'm... nothing got me.

618
00:46:58,604 --> 00:46:58,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet.

619
00:46:59,305 --> 00:47:07,973
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that I feel like I'm a prey to this suffering means the hunter is there, always, and he might pounce at any moment.

620
00:47:08,994 --> 00:47:18,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't necessarily pounce, but you recognize that you are actually a prey in this field, when there are, you know, serious carnivores.

621
00:47:18,843 --> 00:47:23,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if they don't kill you, the fact that they might kill you, that is suffering in itself, that concern, that fear.

622
00:47:26,289 --> 00:47:28,832
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, I am subject to suffering.

623
00:47:28,852 --> 00:47:36,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I don't necessarily suffer, but I recognize that if suffering does land, at some point, I'm subjected to it.

624
00:47:36,401 --> 00:47:37,623
[SPEAKER_01]: I won't be able to prevent that.

625
00:47:37,963 --> 00:47:39,225
[SPEAKER_01]: I might be able to deal with it.

626
00:47:40,950 --> 00:47:44,273
[SPEAKER_01]: As an aftermath, in hindsight, I might be able to resolve it, whatever.

627
00:47:44,533 --> 00:47:47,995
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you were touched in the first place, that is THE problem.

628
00:47:48,276 --> 00:47:48,756
[SPEAKER_01]: One and only.

629
00:47:48,816 --> 00:47:49,156
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

630
00:47:49,597 --> 00:47:50,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha said.

631
00:47:50,477 --> 00:47:55,481
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing he teaches, the only thing he could possibly teach, is suffering and freedom from it.

632
00:47:55,641 --> 00:47:59,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everything else is done because of that, whether people know it or not.

633
00:48:01,125 --> 00:48:04,888
[SPEAKER_01]: You pursue pleasure because not having enough of it is not pleasant.

634
00:48:05,777 --> 00:48:07,418
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, obviously, you realize you never have enough of it.

635
00:48:07,438 --> 00:48:08,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you keep pursuing more.

636
00:48:08,858 --> 00:48:10,499
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's just more and more unpleasant.

637
00:48:10,879 --> 00:48:11,819
[SPEAKER_01]: You run away from pain.

638
00:48:11,879 --> 00:48:12,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

639
00:48:12,159 --> 00:48:12,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's unpleasant.

640
00:48:15,020 --> 00:48:15,620
[SPEAKER_00]: You're running away.

641
00:48:15,640 --> 00:48:16,141
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pleasant.

642
00:48:17,321 --> 00:48:18,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah.

643
00:48:18,941 --> 00:48:20,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you avoided it temporarily.

644
00:48:20,842 --> 00:48:21,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like temporary.

645
00:48:21,742 --> 00:48:24,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Temporary means there's obviously no greater perspective than the wisdom.

646
00:48:25,804 --> 00:48:28,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Because scratching your itch temporarily relieves you.

647
00:48:28,405 --> 00:48:32,106
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the long run, it makes you much more infected and worse.

648
00:48:37,388 --> 00:48:46,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people would say that people are much more distracted nowadays, but, you know, with phones or whatever, Internet, I don't believe that.

649
00:48:46,630 --> 00:48:47,390
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a different type.

650
00:48:47,670 --> 00:48:51,051
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's just that it's more proliferated, but the roots were the same.

651
00:48:51,371 --> 00:48:55,372
[SPEAKER_01]: The roots of distraction, they're rooted in the same things.

652
00:48:56,372 --> 00:49:04,854
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people today are more frivolous, probably more divorced from the nature of things.

653
00:49:06,260 --> 00:49:07,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that proliferation.

654
00:49:08,160 --> 00:49:11,721
[SPEAKER_01]: But that nature is still there, which means the roots of the distractions are still the same.

655
00:49:16,422 --> 00:49:29,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you want to get things going quick, you know, you want to start to practice, you want to see the truth, understand, be able to see.

656
00:49:32,130 --> 00:49:32,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Good.

657
00:49:32,350 --> 00:49:33,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Precepts, restraint.

658
00:49:33,631 --> 00:49:37,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Precepts, restraint, and then we'll show you the fear quite quickly.

659
00:49:37,873 --> 00:49:39,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Put down your phone, switch off the internet.

660
00:49:40,054 --> 00:49:41,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Put down your phone, switch off the internet.

661
00:49:41,835 --> 00:49:46,198
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're a layman, well, try and be a celibate, if not, try and keep the third precept.

662
00:49:46,678 --> 00:49:47,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't eat like a pig.

663
00:49:47,899 --> 00:49:49,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Restrain yourself.

664
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:56,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, start practicing restraint in regard to your senses, and all these things will be revealed very quickly.

665
00:49:58,125 --> 00:49:58,545
[SPEAKER_01]: And then,

666
00:50:00,294 --> 00:50:01,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And be very careful.

667
00:50:01,898 --> 00:50:03,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't start holding views on account of it.

668
00:50:04,564 --> 00:50:06,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And holding views on account of it is what I said.

669
00:50:06,989 --> 00:50:08,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby, you know that you don't know.

670
00:50:10,213 --> 00:50:12,954
[SPEAKER_01]: But you accepted that what you're doing now is the right thing.

671
00:50:13,014 --> 00:50:15,795
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you develop a belief that this is the right thing to do.

672
00:50:16,015 --> 00:50:18,355
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still don't know for yourself that it's the right thing to do.

673
00:50:18,695 --> 00:50:25,337
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said that, you know, monks can live withdrawn from sensuality, physically and even mentally.

674
00:50:25,617 --> 00:50:29,638
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, they end up arguing and debating and holding on to views.

675
00:50:29,738 --> 00:50:31,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, māra still controls them.

676
00:50:34,500 --> 00:50:39,241
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, one thing at a time, you know, you can't be dealing with the views,

677
00:50:40,176 --> 00:50:41,817
[SPEAKER_01]: If your behavior is still unrestrained.

678
00:50:42,037 --> 00:50:42,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

679
00:50:42,717 --> 00:50:49,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, restrain behavior, don't pursue distractions, and then endure the views for a while, and then they'll become apparent, and you'll know.

680
00:50:49,820 --> 00:50:52,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you basically, again, what I'm saying, you stop rooting them in unwholesome.

681
00:50:53,681 --> 00:50:54,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

682
00:50:54,102 --> 00:50:56,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, or, you can't help but having views.

683
00:50:57,083 --> 00:51:02,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you just want to know, make sure that you know that when you have views.

684
00:51:03,765 --> 00:51:05,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know that these are inevitable.

685
00:51:06,002 --> 00:51:13,949
[SPEAKER_01]: The views are inevitable, but all I... what I can do is not hold to them blindly to such an extent that I believe, even if it's about the sīla and the dhamma.

686
00:51:15,410 --> 00:51:17,792
[SPEAKER_01]: There's still no justification to hold the views in regard to it.

687
00:51:21,956 --> 00:51:22,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that...

688
00:51:24,028 --> 00:51:25,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, abandoning views.

689
00:51:25,530 --> 00:51:28,713
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a view that nothing pleases me.

690
00:51:28,994 --> 00:51:31,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and that view pleases you.

691
00:51:31,396 --> 00:51:32,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the view.

692
00:51:33,158 --> 00:51:33,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what people say.

693
00:51:34,179 --> 00:51:36,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you don't hold to any fixed views.

694
00:51:36,082 --> 00:51:38,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that is a fixed view that you clearly hold on to.

695
00:51:40,587 --> 00:51:44,431
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't hold to views by recognizing that views are inevitable.

696
00:51:45,054 --> 00:51:45,975
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already okay.

697
00:51:46,135 --> 00:51:48,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do have views, but you don't hold to them.

698
00:51:48,998 --> 00:51:49,799
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what it's meant.

699
00:51:49,819 --> 00:51:53,684
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean, like, don't hold to views, like, not actually have any views.

700
00:51:53,704 --> 00:51:54,404
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

701
00:51:55,105 --> 00:51:56,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the right view is a view.

702
00:51:56,967 --> 00:51:58,589
[SPEAKER_01]: It just replaces the wrong view.

703
00:51:58,629 --> 00:51:59,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the most you can do.

704
00:51:59,650 --> 00:52:00,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's all you can do.

705
00:52:00,711 --> 00:52:02,954
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you need to do.

706
00:52:02,974 --> 00:52:05,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Just replace your wrong views with the right view.

707
00:52:05,857 --> 00:52:07,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will lead you to the other side.

708
00:52:07,038 --> 00:52:09,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Your wrong intention for the rise, isn't it?

709
00:52:09,239 --> 00:52:09,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

710
00:52:09,960 --> 00:52:12,342
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, intentions... because they're all intentions.

711
00:52:12,362 --> 00:52:13,422
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll never intend anything.

712
00:52:13,462 --> 00:52:14,103
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll never do anything.

713
00:52:14,123 --> 00:52:16,564
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll never leave this room for the rest of my life, because everything is bad.

714
00:52:16,765 --> 00:52:17,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's your intention.

715
00:52:17,845 --> 00:52:22,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's something you're clearly doing, you're clearly intending, clearly performing, and you're fully not aware of.

716
00:52:23,289 --> 00:52:25,090
[SPEAKER_00]: But that view, in that sutta, that says...

717
00:52:25,290 --> 00:52:28,212
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's in Majjhima, 74,

718
00:52:30,235 --> 00:52:31,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing pleases me, he says there.

719
00:52:31,675 --> 00:52:32,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you oppose to this?

720
00:52:32,716 --> 00:52:34,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you oppose to denunciation?

721
00:52:34,676 --> 00:52:35,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

722
00:52:35,617 --> 00:52:39,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you, you, you, you would, you would basically, uh, nothing pleases me.

723
00:52:39,498 --> 00:52:42,719
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't commit to any other, more particular view.

724
00:52:43,219 --> 00:52:46,100
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you know, there is that one that you need to deal with.

725
00:52:52,542 --> 00:52:58,584
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, I don't, I'm not obsessed with existence.

726
00:53:00,775 --> 00:53:01,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

727
00:53:02,736 --> 00:53:06,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything that is, you're not, you're not pulled into it anymore.

728
00:53:06,859 --> 00:53:07,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

729
00:53:07,860 --> 00:53:12,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason you were pulled into it was because you were acting out of fear of suffering.

730
00:53:13,143 --> 00:53:13,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

731
00:53:14,624 --> 00:53:17,586
[SPEAKER_01]: You were a prey to suffering and you were just doing what prey does.

732
00:53:17,906 --> 00:53:18,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

733
00:53:22,169 --> 00:53:25,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, there might be a hunter behind this tree.

734
00:53:26,732 --> 00:53:28,633
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know, why are you dressed around?

735
00:53:30,813 --> 00:53:39,955
